// A BLANKET BOUND OVER A PACKAGE-LOCAL HOMONYM TRAIT.
//
// `Hash` is the collision: `logos.lang.hash::Hash` owns the bare registry slot,
// so THIS package's `Hash` is filed under `trait_ident_homonym_bound::Hash`
// (B-mv-02). Mono now keys its impl facts by that IDENTITY
// (impl_keys::CANONICAL_TRAIT → Mono::concrete_impls_, a pair), while a BOUND
// still reaches mono as bare TEXT. This file is the shape where those two meet.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It was written to be the SENSOR for mono.cpp's
// bare-spelling alias insert, and it is NOT one — MEASURED, both arms: with
// the alias disabled it is still green, because sema (not mono's fact table)
// decides which concretes a blanket admits. An earlier attempt, a plain
// generic bound `fn tag_of<T: Hash>(x: T) -> i64 { x.tag() }`, was likewise
// green with the alias off. The alias survives as a behaviour-preserving
// hedge with no witness, and mono.cpp says so at the insert. Do not cite this
// file as evidence that the alias is load-bearing.
//
// ⚠ WHAT IT DID EXPOSE — HALF NOW CLOSED (B-mv-03), HALF NOT, AND THE SPLIT IS
// MEASURED. The bound was conflated on the SEMA side: `impl<T: Hash> Marker for
// T` admitted the stdlib `Hash`'s concretes (`i32`, `i64`, …) into `Marker` as
// well as this package's `S`.
//
//   · CLOSED, and ASSERTED next door. A bound over this package's `Hash` no
//     longer admits a type that implements only the stdlib `Hash`. `impls_` now
//     ALSO files each impl under the trait's IDENTITY key
//     (`pkg::Trait::Target`, SemaChecker::collect_impl), and a bound carries the
//     identity it denoted WHERE IT WAS WRITTEN (TraitBound::canonical_trait,
//     captured in SemaChecker::read_trait_bound_args) — so
//     `SemaChecker::check_type_bounds` and `SemaChecker::sema_has_impl_recursive`
//     ask for one homonym and MISS the other. `hmid_tag(5i64)` and
//     `call_marker(1i32)` are now REFUSALS with a ground naming the trait; the
//     door is `tests/logos/fail/trait_ident_homonym_bound_refused.logos`.
//     ⚠ THE ADDITIVE ALIAS IS MECHANICALLY CHECKED, not asserted in a comment:
//     `SemaChecker::check_impl_registry_key_identity` (called from `run`) aborts
//     if an impl is ever filed under only one of the two keys. Disabling the
//     alias insert makes THIS file abort the compiler, rc 134 — measured.
//
//   · NOT CLOSED, and that is why the blanket body still does not call `tag()`.
//     With the body written as `return self.tag();` this program STILL dies at
//     `'func.call' op 'i32__tag' does not reference a valid function` — measured
//     after B-mv-03, and measured again with mono's concrete bare alias
//     DISABLED: byte-identical, so the alias is not the admitter either. Nothing
//     in this TU calls `Marker` with an `i32`; MONO instantiates the blanket
//     over every type its own fact table says implements `Hash`, and mono's
//     blanket bound reaches it as RAW TEXT — `mono_has_impl_recursive` /
//     `mono_concrete_satisfies_bound` never pass through
//     `resolve_trait_query_name`. Closing that needs the identity MIRRORED to
//     LIR (a `canonical_bound_trait` twin of the existing
//     `ImplView::canonical_trait()`, plus the same on `push_tbound`); it is a
//     format-touching addition and it is NOT in the B-mv-03 slice. This file
//     therefore still does NOT assert that shape.
//
// What it therefore pins, and it is worth pinning: with the fact table keyed
// by trait identity, a package-local homonym trait still carries a working
// blanket and its own impl still dispatches. A canonicalisation that dropped
// the local trait's facts entirely would red here. That is now the LIVE
// control on B-mv-03 — the cheapest wrong way to make the refusal next door
// go green is to stop finding the local trait's impls at all, and this file is
// what says no.
//
// ⚠ LINK ORDER IS PART OF THE GROUND. Single-TU, no `-l` archive: the stdlib's
// `logos.lang.hash::Hash` is collected first and owns the bare registry slot,
// so THIS package's `Hash` is the pkg-qualified homonym. The MIRROR order — an
// archive's homonym owning the bare slot and the STDLIB's being qualified — is
// `tests/logos/pass/trait_ident_pkg_chain.logos`, and B-mv-03 does NOT close
// the bound defect there: `hmid_tag(5i64)` in that TU still reaches the MLIR
// verifier (measured). The reason is structural, not an oversight — the raw
// `Trait::Target` key is RETAINED on purpose so the ~50 bare-text stdlib gates
// (`Drop::`, `Copy::`, `Index::`, `StableLayout::`, the prefix-strip sweeps)
// keep resolving, and that retained key is where the union still lives. Closing
// the mirror order means converting those gates to ask by identity too.
package trait_ident_homonym_bound;

extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
fn printi(fmt: str, v: i32) { unsafe { printf(fmt.as_ptr(), v); } }

pub trait Hash { fn tag(&self) -> i64; }

pub struct S { pub a: i32, }
impl Hash for S { fn tag(&self) -> i64 { return 91i64; } }

pub trait Marker { fn m(&self) -> i64; }
impl<T: Hash> Marker for T { fn m(&self) -> i64 { return 91i64; } }

fn call_marker<U: Marker>(x: U) -> i64 { return x.m(); }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let v: i64 = call_marker(S { a: 1i32 });
    let d: i64 = S { a: 1i32 }.tag();
    printi("tag=%d\n", v as i32);
    printi("direct=%d\n", d as i32);
    if v != 91i64 { return 41; }
    if d != 91i64 { return 42; }
    return 0i32;
}
